# content: — grepping inside files, driven through the real window.
#
# What this covers that no unit test can: the same queries going through the *live scan*. A harness
# run is unelevated, so there is no MFT index and the first search of a subtree falls to
# FileSystemWalker — which is exactly where a content pass wired only into the index branch would
# return the right rows in every test and nothing at all in the app.
#
# The fixture is built so the load-bearing assertion is possible at all:
#   haystack.txt        contains "the annual report"          -> a hit, on its contents
#   annual report.md    named for it, never says the words    -> NOT a hit
# Those two together are what prove the reader ran rather than the name term quietly answering.

tree .
content-fixture
refresh
go Content
assert-count 6

# --- a phrase is a phrase ---
#
# decoy.txt has both words and never adjacently, so it separates a phrase from two words. This
# query also could not be typed before: a quoted *value* used to make the whole token literal, so
# content:"..." fell through to searching for a file called that.

search content:"annual report"
assert-flattened
assert-error
assert-row haystack.txt
assert-row code.cs
assert-row utf16.txt
assert-no-row decoy.txt
assert-no-row opaque.bin
assert-no-row annual report.md
assert-count 3
shot 01-phrase

# The matching line, with its number — the thing that makes a grep result actionable rather than a
# list of filenames, and the only assertion that reaches the snippet through a rendered cell.
assert-match haystack.txt the annual report is late
assert-match code.cs // the annual report goes here

# --- it is the contents, never the name ---
#
# "annual report.md" is named exactly for the needle. A single hit here would mean the name term
# was answering and the reader had never run.

search content:"annual report" ext:md
assert-count 0

# --- a binary is not text ---
#
# opaque.bin spells the needle in plain ASCII behind a NUL. Matching it would mean the sniff had
# stopped working, and a source-tree search would fill up with .exe string tables.

search content:report
assert-no-row opaque.bin

# --- case is ignored, in both directions ---

search content:ANNUAL
assert-row haystack.txt
search content:annual
assert-row haystack.txt

# --- the floor, and the refusals ---
#
# One character would grep a whole tree for "a", so it is a message rather than a search. Keeping
# the banner up is what leaves the previous results on screen instead of falling back to the
# directory listing for a keystroke.

search content:a
assert-error content
assert-flattened

# An archive has no file on disk behind an entry, and an unread candidate counts as a possible
# match by design — so answering this would return every entry in every container.
search content:report in:archives
assert-error archive

# --- a name term still narrows a content search ---

search haystack content:"annual report"
assert-count 1
assert-row haystack.txt

# --- an ordinary search is untouched, and shows no Match column ---

search report
assert-error
assert-row annual report.md
assert-no-row haystack.txt
assert-count 1
shot 02-ordinary

clear-search
assert-not-flattened
assert-count 6

assert-not-launched
state
